WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.000
How are you doing on your presentation?

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:06.050
Fine. I’m just about to work on the ending 

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.100
and finish the presentation

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.000
The conclusion is very important 

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.200
because it’s the last part of the presentation

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:16.100
It should be short and impressive. 

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.000
I totally agree

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.130 
That’s why I want to wrap up

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:23.160
with a powerful conclusion

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:25.200 
Do you have any suggestions?

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.300
Sure. When you are ready to give your conclusion

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.200 
you could signal the audience by saying

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:35.100 
“That brings me to the end of the presentation.” 

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.200
Or “That’s all the time I have.”

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:41.020
And the audience will know it’s about time

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:42.300 
to conclude the presentation.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.100
Yes. Then you should repeat the main points

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.300 
and reinforce what you have already said

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.100
I can sum up the core message

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:53.120 
for the audience to remember

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.050
Have you heard of the use of “bookends”

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:57.300
in a presentation? 

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:00.210
Isn’t a bookend something you put

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:03.300
at the ends of books to hold them up?

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.230
Right. So to “bookend” a presentation

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:10.160 
means to place the first “bookend” in the opening

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:13.040 
and the second “bookend” at the end

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:16.050 
For instance, use a quote in the opening

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.100
and then end with another quote

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:21.200
Or ask a question in the beginning

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.220 
and give the answer at the end

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.100
Right. Tell a story during the opening

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.300
but hold the ending until the conclusion

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.100 
This kind of “bookend” presentation

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:34.200 
will bring the ending back to the opening 

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:36.080
and complete a circle

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.100 
It will result in a more memorable ending

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.100
That’s a very smart idea

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:45.200
By doing this, we can remind the audience

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:47.210 
when we first introduced the topic

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.190 
and what we expected them to learn

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.040
from the presentation

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:54.190
And then the conclusion is not

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.240
only a review of the main points

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:59.040
but also a checklist to see

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:01.090
if you covered the entire message

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:04.100 
you wanted to convey to the audience.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.100
Wonderful

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:08.300
I’ll go back and start working on my conclusion right away